  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Designing the App Container**\n\nOkay, I\'m now focusing on implementing the `/src/App.tsx` container. I\'m carefully considering its layout and state management. The plan is to handle the default state, which will load `settings` from `localStorage` or default to `DEFAULT_SETTINGS`. I\'m also planning to incorporate `historyLogs`.\n\n\n**Defining Initial State Values**\n\nI\'ve specified the initial state values. `sensorData` defaults to coordinates with `beta: 0`, `gamma: 0`, and `alpha: 0` when using the simulator. The initial `timeLeft` is set to 25 minutes, with `isTimerRunning` as `false`. I\'m defining `sensorPermissionState` and `activeTab` with appropriate initial values.\n\n\n**Refining State Logic**\n\nI\'m now refining the state flow in `App.tsx`. The core idea is to compute classified states dynamically from current sensor data. Using `classifyDeviceState` provides a clean way to update `activeFaceState` and `activeDirection` directly from `sensorData`. I\'m working through the logic for how tilted directions are handled, and how we handle the `lastTiltedDirection` state, so the timer properly continues or pauses.\n\n\n**Implementing the Tilt Logic**\n\nI\'ve nailed the tilt logic in `App.tsx`! The plan hinges on `classifyDeviceState`, which gives us `activeFaceState` directly. We use `useEffect` with `classifyDeviceState` to monitor tilt data. If a cardinal direction changes, it updates `activeDirection` \342\200\223 a perfect cube/hexagonal timer! Flat faces keep the previous value, as needed. Now, let\'s think about saving the state.\n\n\n**Saving History Logs**\n\nI\'m now focusing on implementing the history log functionality. Completed logs will be saved when the timer reaches zero, using `addHistoryLog`. This will prepend new logs to the existing list, which is then saved to `localStorage`. I\'m also planning to support manual saving, clearing, and the calculation of statistics based on these logs.\n\n**Crafting the App UI**\n\nI\'m now immersed in the UI design for `App.tsx`, and planning a responsive layout. There\'s a top bar with a visual timer logo and tagline, and a main split container. Desktop will feature a left panel for the simulator, a central clock, and a right panel with stats/settings tabs. Mobile view adapts to show the relevant panel, toggled by a bottom navigation bar.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will now implement the orchestrator component `/src/App.tsx` which manages user state, handles browser local storage, monitors orientation sensors, manages iOS permission prompts, and renders a bento-grid dashboard on desktop and a smooth bottom-tab navigator on mobile."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 8
      nanos: 554626726
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
