  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Envisioning Interface Dynamics**\n\nI\'m now picturing how the main interface should respond to directional changes. I\'m focusing on dynamic transformations; specifically, how the visuals can convey rolling and collision effects based on the perceived direction. This explores interactive visual feedback.\n\n\n**Designing Dynamic Elements**\n\nI\'ve got a clearer vision now. The central idea is to have elements within the main interface, like the GravityTimer, dynamically react to the phone\'s orientation. Think of simulated rolling, collisions, and gravity effects for small objects inside the timer visualizer or even within the PhoneSimulator. This should make the interaction feel incredibly interactive, gamified, and delightful. I\'m exploring the idea of using particles like sand grains, beads, or themed emoji icons that respond to beta and gamma angles.\n\n\n**Implementing Physics Logic**\n\nI\'m now diving into the implementation details for the dynamic elements. Specifically, I\'m leaning toward a React-based 2D physics simulation within the `GravityTimer` component. This involves using `sensorData` (beta, gamma, alpha, `usingSimulator`) to derive a 2D gravity vector (`gx`, `gy`). These represent the tilt values in device space, which will drive the visual response of the simulated objects. I am considering light-weight Framer Motion or CSS translations with spring physics.\n\n\n**Calculating Gravity Vector**\n\nI\'m now refining the gravity vector calculations for the dynamic elements. Specifically, I\'m defining the relationship between tilt angles (beta, gamma) and the resulting acceleration in the x and y directions. I\'m focusing on the conversion of tilt degrees to a gravity vector, and plan to use this to drive the movement of particles within the timer visualizer. I am considering sin functions to map beta and gamma angles to ax and ay.\n\n\n**Simulating Particle Movement**\n\nI\'m now implementing the particle movement logic using `sensorData.gamma`. I\'m updating particle positions based on velocity, acceleration, and damping. I am considering constraining particles within a circular boundary and implementing velocity reflection and position capping. I\'m focusing on the visual impact of sand or beads piling up due to the device\'s tilt, envisioning a stunning visual effect!\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 8
      nanos: 891782200
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
