    thinking: "**Analyzing the Timer Structure**\n\nI\'ve been looking closely at the core structure for the main timer display. Specifically, I\'m examining that `<div` element with all the nested classes. It\'s built to be very flexible, and the SVG with the circle is inside. I\'m focusing on how this layout handles responsive scaling.\n\n\n**Envisioning Particle Physics**\n\nI\'m now deeply engrossed in envisioning the particle physics for the timer display\'s visual upgrade. The plan involves a canvas within the absolute overlay container, centered and sized to match the circular display. This allows for dynamic particle rendering: small, round, subtly colored beads exhibiting realistic bouncing, rolling, and collisions, both amongst themselves and with the circular boundary. A new component, `GravityParticlesCanvas.tsx`, will be the dedicated handler.\n\n\n**Implementing Particle Collisions**\n\nI\'m now implementing elastic collisions between the particles and the circular boundary! The goal is a highly realistic feel. The plan includes subtle visual feedback for collisions: \"tick\" animations, ripples, or soft sound effects if enabled. I\'m also considering adding mouse/touch drag interaction to add user input to the simulation, which might look cool. I\'m also planning to adjust particle colors based on the active mode (e.g., warm sunset for Pomodoro).\n\n\n"
