  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Deriving Gravity Vector**\n\nOkay, I\'m working on deriving the gravity vector `(gx, gy)` from the device\'s `beta` (pitch) and `gamma` (roll) angles within the 2D clock face of `GravityTimer`. This gravity pull is oriented toward the lowest edge of the physical screen when the device is tilted. This is the current focus of my thoughts.\n\n\n**Validating Orientation Mapping**\n\nOkay, the logic for mapping beta and gamma angles to the gravity vector `(gx, gy)` seems sound. The sine function aligns perfectly with how gravity\'s direction changes relative to the screen. For portrait up, portrait down, landscape right and left configurations, the `sin` of the angle maps correctly to the expected `gy` and `gx` directions. This verification step is complete.\n\n\n**Confirming Flat Surface Behavior**\n\nI\'ve just confirmed the model accurately reflects zero lateral gravity when the device is flat, face up or down. Now I am very happy! This also means the beads will behave correctly on flat surfaces! Finally, I am ready to move on, and start designing a high-fidelity 2D physics simulation to drive the behavior of the gravity particles. I am looking forward to this part! I have a plan to implement it.\n\n\n**Developing Physics Simulation**\n\nI\'m now focused on the high-fidelity 2D circle-to-circle physics simulation for the gravity particles. I envision an elegant, performant implementation in around 150 lines of TypeScript, running at 60fps without external dependencies. The canvas will be `300x300`, scaled with CSS. I\'m planning for 40 particles within a circle of radius `120`, centered at `(150, 150)`. To avoid obscuring the countdown text, the particles can be translucent, styled to appear behind the countdown, or initialized near the edge.\n\n\n**Designing Visual Layering**\n\nI\'m now completely thrilled with the visual layering concept. Placing the canvas behind the text and within the circle creates a beautiful effect! We can achieve this with a background for the circle, canvas with sliding beads behind the text, and SVG progress ring overlapping the edges, all overlaid by high-contrast charcoal text. I\'ll use z-index to manage the layering, ensuring readability and visual appeal. I\'m focusing on the canvas element, and styling the particles.\n\nI\'m now refining the properties of the particles. I\'ll give them random radii between 5px and 8px to mimic pebbles or beads. Each particle\'s color will be derived from the active mode\'s color scheme, ensuring a cohesive theme. I think this will look great!\n\n\n**Implementing Color Transitions**\n\nThe color scheme for the beads, derived from the active mode\'s palette (e.g., rose, emerald, blue, or purple/amber), is crucial. This will enable smooth, mode-based color transitions, either immediately or over time, triggered by the device\'s tilt. I\'m focusing on those colors now!\n\nI am now focusing on the particle-to-particle collision resolution. The algorithm will iterate through all pairs of particles to calculate and resolve collisions.\n\n\n**Refining Collision Response**\n\nI\'ve been working on a fast and accurate particle-to-particle collision algorithm. I\'m leveraging the radii of the beads to resolve overlaps and simulate elastic collisions. I\'m now realizing that using the square of the radius provides an even better approximation for mass! This should make the system very responsive and realistic.\n\n\n**Enhancing Particle Interaction**\n\nI\'m now integrating friction and elasticity into the particle physics. I\'m excited; using `p1.radius * p1.radius` for mass feels incredibly tactile. I\'ve added a friction factor of `0.98` on velocity update, and elasticity set to `0.5` for gentle bounces. This should make the simulation feel very responsive. I\'m also planning to add a mouse/touch repulsion force to allow users to interact with the beads! I\'ll track mouse position relative to the canvas and apply a repulsion force if the mouse is down, using a force proportional to the distance.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 31
      nanos: 760493225
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
